{
  "id": "sort-list",
  "questionId": "sort-list",
  "slug": "sort-list",
  "title": "Sort List",
  "difficulty": "Hard",
  "category": "Fast slow pointers",
  "patterns": [
    "fast & slow pointers",
    "linked list",
    "merge sort"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "an array can be merge-sorted by splitting at the midpoint - what's the linked-list-native way to find that midpoint without random access to indices?",
    "use fast/slow pointers to split the list into two roughly equal halves, being careful to actually cut the link between them",
    "recursively sort each half the same way, down to base cases of length 0 or 1, which are already sorted",
    "merge the two sorted halves the same way you'd merge two sorted arrays: repeatedly take the smaller of the two current front values"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "using an O(n^2) approach (like repeated insertion or selection sort) when O(n log n) is required",
    "forgetting to actually cut the link between the two halves after finding the middle, which can cause the recursion to never terminate or the merge to misbehave",
    "getting the merge step's tie-breaking or termination wrong, dropping the tail of whichever half runs out last"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A singly linked list is given to you as an array `nums`, where `nums[i]`\nis the value of the i-th node in order.\n\n**Sort the list's values in ascending order and return the resulting list's values in order, in O(n log n) time.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= nums.length <= 5 * 10^4",
      "-10^5 <= nums[i] <= 10^5"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "an empty list",
      "a single-node list",
      "a list that is already sorted, or sorted in reverse",
      "duplicate values"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [4, 2, 1, 3]",
        "output": "[1, 2, 3, 4]",
        "expectedOutput": "[1, 2, 3, 4]",
        "explanation": "sorted ascending"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [-1, 5, 3, 4, 0]",
        "output": "[-1, 0, 3, 4, 5]",
        "expectedOutput": "[-1, 0, 3, 4, 5]",
        "explanation": "sorted ascending, including a negative value"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = []",
        "output": "[]",
        "expectedOutput": "[]",
        "explanation": "an empty list is already sorted"
      }
